WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Chapter one showed why a membrane protein keeps its direction during traffic.

2
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.020
But lipids are not locked in place.

3
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.640
How does a cell keep different lipids on the two faces while molecules still move?

4
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.890
A red blood cell gives the best-measured example.

5
00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:24.810
PS and PE sit mainly on the cytoplasmic leaflet.

6
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:28.710
PC and sphingomyelin sit mainly outside.

7
00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.630
Sideways drifting does not erase that pattern.

8
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.060
Crossing is different.

9
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.060
A phospholipid's polar head rarely enters the membrane's oily core unaided.

10
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.280
Without help, most phospholipids change leaflets slowly.

11
00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.640
Slow is not zero, so cells spend energy to maintain asymmetry.

12
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:58.093
An ATP-driven flippase selects lipids such as PS and PE and moves them inward.

13
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:01.133
A scramblase does something different.

14
00:01:02.373 --> 00:01:06.213
When switched on, it lets lipids cross both ways without energy.

15
00:01:06.953 --> 00:01:08.833
The two leaflets then mix.

16
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:15.787
Together, slow leakage, pumps, and regulated scrambling create a steady state, not a

17
00:01:15.787 --> 00:01:16.787
permanent arrangement.

18
00:01:18.227 --> 00:01:20.467
Flippases keep restoring selected lipids.

19
00:01:21.807 --> 00:01:24.187
Switching on a scramblase lets the pattern collapse.

20
00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:32.117
Now predict. Suppose every phospholipid slides sideways, but none crosses to the

21
00:01:32.117 --> 00:01:35.677
other leaflet. Will the two faces become mixed?

22
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:47.920
No. Each leaflet becomes well stirred, yet the lipids remain on their original face.

23
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.860
Across and along are independent directions.

24
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.120
Lateral diffusion is that motion along the membrane.

25
00:01:57.233 --> 00:02:02.893
Fluorescence recovery after photobleaching, or FRAP, measures lateral motion.

26
00:02:04.293 --> 00:02:06.513
Fluorescent membrane proteins begin bright.

27
00:02:07.793 --> 00:02:09.733
A brief laser makes one spot dark.

28
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:13.253
Unbleached proteins then diffuse into it.

29
00:02:14.183 --> 00:02:16.603
The recovery curve answers two questions.

30
00:02:18.003 --> 00:02:21.043
How much fluorescence returns gives the mobile fraction.

31
00:02:22.363 --> 00:02:25.703
How quickly it reaches half of that return gives the half-time.

32
00:02:26.903 --> 00:02:30.843
Dark molecules still move; bleaching only removed their fluorescence.

33
00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:34.113
Take the film's computed example.

34
00:02:35.613 --> 00:02:40.663
Fluorescence starts at one hundred, falls to twenty, then plateaus at eighty.

35
00:02:40.663 --> 00:02:43.753
Sixty units returned, but eighty were bleached.

36
00:02:44.893 --> 00:02:49.233
The mobile fraction is sixty divided by eighty, or zero point seven five.

37
00:02:50.517 --> 00:02:53.037
Half of the recoverable increase is thirty.

38
00:02:54.377 --> 00:02:57.257
Add that to twenty, making the halfway level fifty.

39
00:02:58.517 --> 00:03:03.257
The curve reaches fifty at four seconds, so its recovery half-time is four seconds.

40
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:09.800
Under a stated diffusion model, spot size and half-time can estimate a diffusion

41
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:15.740
coefficient. The estimate is approximate because the bleach and membrane must match

42
00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:19.920
that model. Binding or barriers can change recovery.

43
00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:25.650
So a membrane can preserve two different faces while its components move.

44
00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.450
Fast crossing needs a machine; sideways motion does not.

45
00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:37.330
Pumps build lipid asymmetry, scramblases erase it, and FRAP measures lateral

46
00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:37.770
diffusion.
